  "client_view_text": " This will help you understand the process and make adjustments as needed to achieve your perfect cup.\nStep 1: Prepare the Pour-Over Equipment\nGather your pour-over equipment, including the dripper, filter, and coffee grounds. Rinse the filter with hot water to remove any paper taste and preheat the dripper.\nStep 2: Measure and Grind the Coffee\nMeasure out the right amount of coffee for your pour-over, typically around 1:15 to 1:17 coffee-to-water ratio. Grind the coffee beans to a medium-coarse grind, suitable for pour-over brewing.\nStep 3: Heat This will help you understand the process and make adjustments as needed to achieve your perfect cup.\nStep 1: Prepare the Pour-Over Equipment\nGather your pour-over equipment, including the pour-over dripper, a coffee filter, and a cup or carafe to collect the brewed coffee. Rinse the filter with hot water to remove any paper taste and preheat the pour-over dripper.\nStep 2: Measure and Grind the Coffee\nMeasure out the right amount of coffee for your pour-over, typically around 1:15 to 1:17 coffee-to-water ratio. Grind the coffee beans to a medium-coarse grind, suitable for pour-over brewing.\nStep 3: Heat the Water\nHeat water to the ideal temperature for brewing coffee, between 195\u00b0F and 205\u00b0F. Use a thermometer to ensure the water reaches the correct temperature.\nStep 4: Bloom the Coffee\nPlace the ground coffee in the pour-over filter and pour a small amount of hot water over the coffee to saturate it. This is called the \"bloom\" phase, and it helps to release the CO2 from the coffee beans. Allow the coffee to bloom for about 45 seconds.\nStep 5: Pour the Rest of the Water\nAfter the"
